  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:proportional-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every \\(0<\\rho<1\\), the exact worst-instance multiplier within \\(\\mathfrak D_2\\) is \\[\\inf_{\\mu:\\,|\\arg\\max_i\\mu_i|=1}\\frac{\\Gamma_{\\rho,w}(\\mu)}{I_w(\\mu)}=\\min\\left\\{\\rho C,\\ 1+(1-\\rho)\\eta\\right\\}.\\] A best-elimination witness with \\(h\\in\\arg\\min_k C_k\\) attains the first branch. For an \\(e\\in\\arg\\min_k w_k/(1-w_k)\\), two distinct survivors and a sufficiently separated eliminated arm attain the second branch. Consequently \\(\\rho^\\star\\) uniquely maximizes the displayed value and the maximum is \\(G\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\rho",
      "\\mathfrak D_2",
      "\\mu",
      "\\Gamma_{\\rho,w}",
      "w",
      "I_w",
      "C",
      "\\eta",
      "h",
      "\\rho^\\star",
      "G"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:unique-best",
      "def:robust-schedule",
      "thm:finite-branch-exponent",
      "lem:weighted-projection",
      "thm:universal-dominance",
      "def:proportional-design",
      "thm:multi-proportional-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "This theorem remains the exact eliminate-one reduction of the stronger survivor-count frontier.",
    "gap": "The broader top-\\(m\\) theorem closes the former material maximality gap while preserving the original result as \\(m=K-1\\).",
    "consumer": "Existing eliminate-one implementations retain the original optimal schedule and matching witnesses.",
    "proof_tex": "Apply \\(\\text{thm:multi-proportional-frontier}\\) with \\(m=K-1\\) and \\(\\ell=1\\). The specialization identities proved there give\n\\[\n \\mathsf D_{\\rho,w,K-1}=\\mathsf D_{\\rho,w},\\qquad\n \\Gamma_{\\rho,w,K-1}=\\Gamma_{\\rho,w},\\qquad\n C_{K-1}=C,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n \\eta_1=\\frac{\\min_e w_e}{1-\\min_e w_e}\n =\\min_e\\frac{w_e}{1-w_e}=\\eta.\n\\]\nIts exact worst-instance formula therefore becomes\n\\[\n \\inf_{\\mu:\\,|\\arg\\max_i\\mu_i|=1}\n \\frac{\\Gamma_{\\rho,w}(\\mu)}{I_w(\\mu)}\n =\\min\\{\\rho C,1+(1-\\rho)\\eta\\}.\n\\]\nThe best-exclusion construction in that theorem has \\(S=\\mathcal A\\setminus\\{h\\}\\) and is precisely the stated best-elimination witness. Its separated terminal construction has singleton excluded set \\(\\{e\\}\\), with \\(e\\in\\arg\\min_k w_k/(1-w_k)\\), and supplies the stated two-survivor comparison while all other survivor means are sufficiently separated.\n\nFor completeness, \\(C>1\\) by \\(\\text{lem:weighted-projection}\\) and \\(\\eta>0\\) because \\(w\\in\\mathcal W\\). Thus \\(\\rho\\mapsto\\rho C\\) is strictly increasing and \\(\\rho\\mapsto1+(1-\\rho)\\eta\\) is strictly decreasing, and their unique intersection lies in \\((0,1)\\). Solving the intersection equation gives\n\\[\n \\rho^\\star=\\frac{1+\\eta}{C+\\eta},\n \\qquad\n C\\rho^\\star=\\frac{C(1+\\eta)}{C+\\eta}=G.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\rho^\\star\\) is the unique maximizer and the maximum is \\(G\\).",
